  <div class="story__content">
    <p>Some forms of loss do not arrive as a problem to solve. They reshape the landscape of ordinary life, asking the people nearby to listen before they offer a way out.</p>
    <p>Support can be small and immediate: a remembered date, a slow conversation, or a hand extended without an expectation that someone will feel better on schedule.</p>
    <h2>The water around us and the water in us</h2>
    <p>This demo text preserves the cadence, reading width, headline hierarchy, and tool placement of a long-form story without reproducing the source article. Its purpose is to make the UI feel like a live editorial page.</p>
    <p>The essential design choice is room. A narrow column, quiet metadata, and clear breaks make space for a reader to stay with an idea.</p>
    <blockquote>What changes when support becomes company instead of a route back to shore?</blockquote>
    <h2>Building the boat</h2>
    <p>The most useful interface is often the least insistent one. It offers familiar tools at the edges and leaves the center available for the story.</p>
